{
  "name": "scatter_toda",
  "description": "Toda — spoken by the Toda people of the Nilgiri hills in Tamil Nadu; one of the smallest speech communities in the atlas (several hundred to low thousands of speakers).  Toda is famous in phonological literature for the COMPLEXITY of its inventory: a 6-place stop system that distinguishes dental from alveolar (so column 4 dental AND column 5 alveolar are both lit for stops), three sibilants including a unique alveolar /s̟/, a retroflex sibilant /ʂ/, and a post-alveolar /ʃ/, plus voiceless and voiced trills and laterals.  Among the densest inventories in any subcontinental language, documented in Emeneau's *Toda Songs* and subsequent phonological work.  30 cells filled.",
